  "cover_link": "https://static.wikia.nocookie.net/marveldatabase/images/9/94/Fantastic_Four_Vol_1_19.png",
  "plot_summary": "It's the typical 3-pages of \"let's fill up space doing mundane stuff before getting to the meat of the story\" that finally gets us to the last panel of page 3 where Reed lays out why he's gotten everyone together (including Alicia).&nbsp; Reed thinks he saw an old Egyptian artifact that hints of an ancient Pharaoh who was healed of blindness.&nbsp; He wants to go back and see if there's hope for Alicia.\n\nThey head off to the now-abandoned castle of Doctor Doom to use his time machine that he used when they first met back in [LINK:1962:FF_5|FF #5].&nbsp; And sure enough, with Alicia running the controls, they soon find themselves back in ancient Egypt, where they are attacked and have to spend the first couple of pages of their arrival battling soldiers and chariots.\n\nBut suddenly, all their powers seem to fade away, and before they know it, they find themselves in a throne room before the Pharaoh, a man named Rama-Tut, who not only can speak perfect English, he actually <i>knows</i> who the Fantastic Four are!&nbsp; Rama-Tut speaks as though the FF were something from ancient history!&nbsp; Turns out that he has a \"ultra-diode ray, invented in the year 3000\" -- a thousand years farther in the future than even when the FF came from!&nbsp; He, <i>too</i>, is a time traveler!\n\nRama-Tut tells his story about being bored in the year 3000, finding himself a man of action with no adventures to be found anywhere -- \"All was peaceful... horribly, unbearably peaceful!\"&nbsp; By studying ancient films, he learned of famous adventurers such as the Fantastic Four, always envying them.&nbsp; Then one day, \"while visiting the ruins of an amazing ancestor of mine,\" he came across the ruins of possibly his greatest invention of all -- a <i>time machine</i>!\n\nAfter spending years studying and re-creating the amazing device, he decided to go back to ancient Egypt, being careful to construct it in the form of a Sphinx -- an idol -- something that wouldn't stand out in the time period.&nbsp; Going back to this era and making it his base of operations would allow him to be a \"time looter,\" with the added benefit being that his advanced technology would also allow him to rule over the people there.\n\nHowever, the time machine was damaged upon arrival in Egypt, and he injured his eyes, but because the radiation from his device had also affected \"a rare herb,\" he was able to regain his sight, and because of his advanced weaponry, he had ruled the populace here ever since.&nbsp; But he's also been trapped in the past.\n\nIn the next moment, he aims the \"ultra-diode ray\" at the FF, which saps their will power, and he demands that Sue become his queen.&nbsp; The others are aware of what they are doing but can't resist and serve at Rama-Tut's pleasure.&nbsp; Even Sue is powerless to object to the upcoming wedding.\n\nYet, on the deck of a ship, forced to row with other slaves, the Thing is exposed to the sun (which is \"far hotter than that of twentieth-century New York\"), and its rays temporarily transform him back to Ben Grimm.&nbsp; Not only does this free him from the effects of Tut's weapon, but it also allows him to slip free of his bonds.&nbsp; He makes his way back to the palace and surprises Rama-Tut long enough to grab hold of the Pharaoh's ray gun, use it on Sue to restore her to normal, and toss it to her before he reverts to the Thing and succumbs once again to the will-sapping effects of the weapon.&nbsp; Sue immediately releases Johnny from its effects.\n\nNow facing super-powered enemies <i>without</i> his weapon, Rama-Tut runs for his machine, which is inside the Sphinx.&nbsp; The restored FF pursue, still wanting to find the cure that could help Alicia.&nbsp; Once inside, Rama-Tut is irritated by their interference and strikes back with various defensive obstacles, which the FF are forced to evade.\n\nUltimately tracking Rama-Tut to an inner chamber, they discover a strange device which turns out to be a \"satellite globe\" that allows Tut to head off to \"another dimension, another age...\" (<i>obviously, he's repaired his actual time machine without saying so</i>).\n\nRama-Tut flies off, leaving behind his Sphinx-shaped idol as an everlasting mystery to mankind.&nbsp; That leaves the FF to ponder whether they'll ever see him again, and Reed wonders who the \"ancient ancestor\" might be -- could it be Doctor Doom himself?&nbsp; The Thing even wonders if Rama-Tut actually <i>is</i> Doom, who might have found a way to live for a thousand years.&nbsp; Is he actually the Doom of the future?\n\nMeanwhile, Sue finds a canister labeled \"Optic Nerve Restorative,\" but they are all soon forced to flee because Rama-Tut set all his machinery to blow up, leaving no trace behind of his existence inside the Sphinx.\n\nBy this time, their 24 hours are up, and in the next moment, they are retrieved by Doom's time platform, which returns them to the 20<sup>th</sup> century, but what's this?&nbsp; The canister is gone!&nbsp; Reed was afraid of that -- it contained radioactive material that Doom's machine refused to transport to and from the past.&nbsp; So their trip was a waste, but at least they know that such a substance exists, and Reed promises to continue to try to duplicate it in his lab.\n\nBut Alicia is just happy they're back safe and sound.",
  "grade_one_liner": "A mission of compassion detours into ancient Egypt and future-tech weirdness.\nAmbitious, brisk, and at times charmingly illogical, it still earns its place in the run.",
  "subtitle": "Past Remedies, Future Complications",
  "review_text": "[ASSET: right | OldPharaohGainsSight | width=21%] The story's central objective is to go back in time in order to investigate how a blind Pharaoh was healed so that he could see, which Reed believes might be just the breakthrough he needs to help Alicia regain her sight.&nbsp; It's a surprisingly grounded motivation for a story that immediately veers into cosmic-level weirdness.\n[BR]\n[BR] This bit of continuity was good to see, since only three issues ago (in [LINK:1963:FF_16|FF #16|2026-09-14]), Ben asked Reed to stop trying to change him back to human form and instead try to help Alicia, so I liked that he hadn't forgotten about his promise to try.\n\nNaturally, the standard set of questions arise, namely\n[HANG] * How can there be a cure for blindness in the past, before the days of any atomic technology?\n[HANG] * How do we even get <i>back</i> to the past?\n[HANG] * Should we dare risk going there?&nbsp; [SPAN: fontsize=small; text=(This never even crosses Reed's mind.)]\n\nAnd as usual with the Fantastic Four, such questions don't slow them down for long, before their trademark mix of confidence and recklessness kicks in almost immediately.\n\nThe answer to their time-travelling question, at least, is to hope that Dr. Doom's time machine (last seen in [LINK:1962:FF_5|FF #5]) is still functional in his \"abandoned castle.\"&nbsp; And as fate (and <i>Comic Book Convenience 101</i>) would have it, the machine still works.&nbsp; Deciding to leave Alicia behind to operate the controls (after \"hours of practice,\" according to page 5), off they go!\n\nThe book isn't even a quarter of the way over, and they've already been transported to ancient Egypt!\n[BR] [SPAN: fontsize=small; text=(This is breakneck pacing at its finest -- almost jarring how quickly the book pivots from heartfelt hope to time-hopping spectacle.)]\n\nDuring the requisite three pages of battle action that ensues (unsurprisingly, when one just randomly appears next to the Sphinx in front of all the local inhabitants), the FF try to ward off an attack -- even the chariots -- but very suddenly, they find their powers mysteriously fading away, and by the bottom of page 8, they're shockingly <i>defeated</i>!\n\nWhat a <b><i>reversal</i></b>!\n[BR] One moment, the FF is basically unstoppable -- the next moment, completely helpless.\n[BR] It's the kind of abrupt shift that feels baked into these early FF stories.\n\n[SPAN: severity=error; fontsize=larger; text=Cue the appearance of Pharaoh Rama-Tut, ruler of the realm!]\n\n[DIVIDER]\nRama-Tut's backstory indicates he's from the year 3,000 and that time machines don't exist there.&nbsp; He was able to create one only because he found some schematics left behind by \"an amazing ancestor\" of his.\n\nI feel obliged to point out that there is no mention made of Zarrko, the \"Tomorrow Man\" from the year 2262 that Thor faced in [LINK:1962:JIM_86_thor0|JIM #86], who invented his own time machine so that he could travel back 300 years to pick up nuclear weapons which he could transport to the future so that he could \"conquer the twenty-third century!\" (see its page 2).\n\nSince that book was published a full 10 months before <i>this</i> one, readers are left to assume that:\n[HANG] * Zarrko's time machine was abandoned as a result of him losing his memory (as shown on its page 13)\n[HANG] * <b>OR</b>... Stan and Jack were simply banking on readers <i>forgetting</i> about this encounter, <i>heh</i>\n\nAs Marvel continuity seems to still be in a primordial soup stage, I suppose contradictions like this are to be expected.&nbsp; <i>Heck</i>, they're practically a <i>feature</i>!&nbsp; At this stage, the universe is still figuring out its own rules.\n\nWhatever the case may be, this story makes it clear that [SPAN: severity=info; text= time machines are <i>rare</i>, even in the future.]\n\nBut what's curious is why Rama-Tut never asks the most obvious question of all, which is:\n[BR] [SPAN: severity=warning; fontsize=larger; text=If the Fantastic Four are from the 20<sup>th</sup> century, how did <i>they</i> get back to ancient Egypt?]\n\nEven after sapping their will power, he <b>never asks the question</b> (which I find amazing).&nbsp; It's the single most important question in the room, and he treats it like background noise.&nbsp; It's almost impressive how <i>thoroughly</i> he sidesteps it.\n\n<b>No</b>, his only thought is making Sue his <i>queen</i>, and the others his slaves:\n[ASSET: center | BecomingSlaves | width=67%]\n[SPAN: fontsize=small; text=(All this is set in motion with typical Silver Age efficiency -- meaning almost <i>immediately</i>.)]\n\n[ASSET: left | KickingOffTheEscape | width=12%] And <i>just</i> as quickly (<i>heh</i>, after only a single <b>page</b> of enslavement), the Thing reverts to his human form (page 13) and kicks off the events that ultimately lead to their escape.\n[BR]\n[BR] (You really do have to laugh at how quickly these stories plow forward -- the plot practically sprints <i>past</i> you.&nbsp; [SPAN: severity=info; text=There's simply <i>no time to waste</i> if you want to appease the Page Count Gods!])\n\n[DIVIDER]\nMost of the rest of the issue shows how the team breaks free of Tut's will-sapping device and their eventual pursuit of him into the Sphinx, where he very conveniently slips away, destroying all evidence of him ever being there.&nbsp; The Sphinx itself remains, \"to mystify mankind for centuries to come!\"\n\n[SPAN: severity=info; text=And now we know the actual origin of the Sphinx.]\n[BR] [SPAN: fontsize=small; text=(It's a classic Silver Age reset button -- dramatic, convenient, and never questioned.)]\n\n[ASSET: right | WhoWasRamaTut | width=15%] After Johnny concludes that they'll never see him again, Reed isn't so sure.&nbsp; He and Ben speculate on who this ancestor might have been.&nbsp; Ben even wonders if Rama-Tut might actually be a future version of Doctor Doom himself.\n[BR]\n[BR] This is a strange notion, especially when Rama-Tut indicated (on page 10, during his backstory monologue) how he \"envied your dramatic careers!\"&nbsp; I'm sorry, but even in the far-flung future, that <i>hardly</i> sounds like something Dr. Doom would ever do!&nbsp; [SPAN: severity=warning; text=He doesn't <i>envy</i>!&nbsp; <b>He declares superiority by default.</b>]\n\n[ASSET: left | LabeledInEnglish | width=14%] Meanwhile, Sue finds what they actually came for -- the canister of \"Optic Nerve Restorative\" (<i>conveniently labeled in perfect English</i>).\n[BR]\n[BR] But sadly, because it had been bathed in radiation, when they return to the 20<sup>th</sup> century, Doom's machine filters it out, and it does not survive the trip.\n\nIt seems their entire excursion to the past has been entirely in vain!\n[ASSET: center | AllForNothing | width=67%]\n\n[SPAN: severity=warning; fontsize=larger; fonttype=bold-italic; text=Alas!]\n\n[SPAN: fontsize=small; text=Coming home empty-handed?&nbsp; Not even a <i>symbolic</i> win.]\n[BR] [SPAN: fontsize=small; text=The day isn't even <i>saved</i> this time!&nbsp; <b><i>Bummer</i></b><i>!</i>]\n\n[BOTTOM_LINE]\nLet's give Stan and Jack some serious kudos here for the interesting and novel twist of having the Fantastic Four go back in time, only to meet someone from the <b>future</b>, <i>another</i> time traveler.&nbsp; Even though the cover <i>does</i> actually reveal this (plainly stating that Pharaoh Rama-Tut is from the future), it's still a fresh idea, and <i>certainly </i>not one you'd expect.\n\nMore kudos for the quick follow-up on Reed's promise to Ben to try to find a cure for Alicia's blindness, and we're shown here how seriously he's taken this to heart, even to the point of noticing hints of a possible cure on ancient Egyptian hieroglyphics.\n\nI also liked how Reed and Ben pondered who the \"amazing ancestor\" of Rama-Tut was.&nbsp; Including this brief bit of speculation in the dialogue showed a remarkable bit of forethought, as if Stan somehow sensed it would be batted around in the letters pages to come, and he wanted to get on top of it, even perhaps to put forward his own thoughts on the matter.\n\nWhoever the mysterious ancestor is, it leaves the reader with something to ponder, and that's never a bad thing for a periodical like this, so [SPAN: severity=success; fonttype=bold; text=great job there], especially when the mythology is still being built issue by issue.\n\nThese are all positives which argue for a high grade, but some hiccups along the way prevent it from scoring higher.&nbsp; The quickness of the story really isn't one of them, as it was just told with the typical brevity of all its contemporaries, so no points off for that.\n\nThat being said, you just have to ask:\n[BR] [SPAN: severity=warning; text=Who leaves a functional time machine just lying around for anyone to find?]\n[BR] [SPAN: fontsize=small; text=(You'd think that such an important topic would be covered in the <i>Super-Villain Handbook</i>.)]\n\nAnd... <i>um...</i> do you <i>really</i> want a blind person operating the controls of your only way back to the present?\n[BR] [SPAN: fontsize=small; text=(I don't know, man, that seems a bit sketchy...)]\n\nRama-Tut's <b><i>complete disinterest</i></b> in how the FF got back to the past is also a knock against the book's logic.&nbsp; It's the kind of oversight that only works because the story never slows down long enough for anyone to question it ([SPAN: severity=info; text=that's <i>my</i> job]).\n\nBut I'll at least give the story a pass on the nicely-labeled \"Optic Nerve Restorative\" canister (despite chuckling at it -- <i>hey</i>, maybe they still speak English in the year 3,000 -- who knows?).\n\nEven with the bumps, [SPAN: severity=success; fonttype=bold; text=the issue's ambition carries real weight], and in this case, the novelties win out enough to keep this as a solid upper-mid-grade read.\n\nIt wasn't perfect, but it was interesting -- and sometimes, that's exactly what an FF adventure needs to be.",
  "side_note": [
    "[HANG0] * The word \"Pharaoh\" is once again misspelled \"Pharoah\" on the cover, in the title and throughout the magazine.&nbsp; Seeing as how I was originally reading a corrected reprint until I later discovered a scan of an original, I had missed this until I happened to notice the original cover and went back to check!\n[BR]\n[BR] For the record, this is the primary reason I now only read scans of <i>original</i> copies, provided I can find them.\n\n[HANG0] * Page 18 shows the Thing pressuring one of the locals for information on where Rama-Tut has fled, which he is able to provide.&nbsp; But the Thing had expressed his surprise back on page 9 that the Pharaoh was speaking to them in English, \"before the language was even <i>invented</i>!\"&nbsp; (And yet, this isn't the first time that the Silver Age has shown us that, no matter who you are, or where you're from... <i>everyone always speaks English</i>!)"
  ],
  "letters_page": "I got the biggest kick out of Sidney D. Evans' short letter which reads:&nbsp; \"Dear Stan and Jack, I told my mother that you two read every letter you get whether it's printed or not.&nbsp; She said, and I quote, 'I'll bet secretaries read and answer them!'\"&nbsp; Marvel's response was:&nbsp; <i>We wish we HAD \"secretaries,\" Sid!&nbsp; We've got one overworked little gal who opens the letters for us, and then we read each and every one -- honest!</i>  (This seems to be the first mention of the \"gal Friday\" who ultimately becomes known as \"Fabulous Flo.\")\n\nEddie L. Malone's letter about his sister bashing him about buying \"a stupid mag named F.F.\" made me laugh when we learn he let his sister read it.&nbsp; The letter ends with, \"Now I don't have to buy F.F. -- my sister beats me to it!\"&nbsp; (Naturally, the editor asks why he can't <i>also</i> buy one, <i>heh</i>.)\n\nMrs. Jo Shelby asks that the Thing be turned back to Ben Grimm permanently so that she doesn't have to keep explaining to her daughter what \"cosmic rays\" are when she inevitably asks every time, \"Mommy, what's THAT?\"\n\nNone other than future Marvel contributor Steve Gerber (co-creator of Howard the Duck) writes in to provide his comments on both the good and bad of the FF, getting the response of:&nbsp; <i>Steve would be a great letter writer if he wasn't so shy about giving opinions!</i>",
  "decoder_ring": "This story is revisited almost 19 years later in <i>Doctor Strange</i>, volume 2, issue 53, where we learn some additional facts about the events of this issue.",
  "highlight": "The FF's chase for a cure in ancient Egypt runs them headlong into Rama‑Tut and future-pharaoh madness."
}